# Housing Intent Engine - Development Rules

## Tech Stack
- Language: Python 3.11+ (FastMCP framework, `mcp<2`)
- Query Engine: DuckDB / Polars (optimized for local Parquet processing)
- Transport: Local stdio MCP server

## Primary Datasets & Connectors
1. Forest Research MCP (`https://mcp.exploreforest.ai/mcp`) - Opinion & sentiment analysis
2. Foundations Planning Parquet (`https://foreman.house-london.uk/download/parquet/`) - Decision delays, doc count, approval rates
3. Foundations API (`https://foreman.house-london.uk/api/v1/applications/`) - Paginated JSON, same data as Parquet
4. WhereToBuild (`data/wheretobuild.parquet`) - Ward/MSOA level housing gap and tightness (event-only, do not publish)
5. UK PlanIt API (`https://www.planit.org.uk/api/`) - Case-level planning application search
6. Planning Data API (`https://www.planning.data.gov.uk/`) - Conservation areas, green belt, brownfield sites

## Foundations column names (do not guess)
- Borough: `area_name` (not `borough`)
- Decision lag: `days_to_decision` (not `decision_days`)
- Documents: `n_documents` (not `document_count`)
- Comments: `n_comments`
- Dwellings: `n_dwellings`
- Outcome: `status` (`Permitted`, `Rejected`, etc.)
- Location: `lat`, `lng`, `ward_name`

## Data Joining Logic
- Join spatial datasets using ONS ward codes or `area_name` / LPA names.
- Prefer coordinate joins over application reference joins (references fail often across datasets).
- Standardize geographic metrics using ONS Open Geography lookups.

## Key Rules & Constraints
- Prefer a local copy at `data/foundations.parquet` for speed; remote HTTP Parquet works but is slow on first query.
- ALWAYS use DuckDB for querying Parquet. Do not load the full dataset into pandas in memory.
- Wrap MCP tools using `@mcp.tool()` from `mcp.server.fastmcp`.
- Return structured dictionaries with quantifiable metrics (`avg_delay_days`, `avg_document_count`, `approval_rate`, etc.).
- Be honest about data limits: Foundations has applications and consultation intensity, not commencements/completions (those are in Planning London Datahub).
